window.docContents[80].resolve({"/Connectivity/#section-spanning-trees":{"contents":"A spanning tree of a connected graph G is a spanning subgraph that is a tree.\nEvery connected graph has a spanning tree. Since T and G are both of type\nSimpleGraph V, the subgraph T ≤ G is automatically spanning (see\nSpanning Subgraphs).\n\nA spanning tree in the Grötzsch graph\n\n#check Connected.exists_isTree_le\n\n\nEvery graph G has a spanning forest (an acyclic spanning subgraph F in which any\npair of vertices is joined by a walk in F iff it is joined by a walk in G).\nNotice that there is no finiteness condition here; the proof uses a maximality\nargument via Zorn's lemma.\n\n#check exists_isAcyclic_reachable_eq_le\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Connectivity\u0009Trees","header":"10.2.5. Spanning Trees","id":"/Connectivity/#section-spanning-trees"},"/Introduction/#section-acknowledgements":{"contents":"This guide could not exist without the work of all the contributors to Lean and\nMathlib.\n\nWe gratefully acknowledge support from the AI for Math Fund, a program of\nRenaissance Philanthropy and founding donor XTX Markets.\n\nAccess to AI tools was provided through Anthropic's Claude for Scientists Team Plan\nand OpenAI's ChatGPT for Academic Researchers.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Introduction","header":"1.5. Acknowledgements","id":"/Introduction/#section-acknowledgements"},"/Maps/#section-maps-of-tensor-products":{"contents":"The two projections Prod.fst and Prod.snd are graph homomorphisms out of\nG ⊗g H. Conversely, a pair of homomorphisms from a common graph K into G and\nH combines into a single homomorphism K →g G ⊗g H. So homomorphisms into\nG ⊗g H are exactly pairs of homomorphisms into G and H. (In the language of\ncategory theory: the tensor product is the product in the category of simple graphs\nand homomorphisms.)\n\nThe first projection is a graph homomorphism G ⊗g H →g G./-- The first projection is a graph homomorphism G ⊗g H →g G. -/\ndef tensorProd.fstHom {V W : Type*} {G : SimpleGraph V} {H : SimpleGraph W} :\n    G ⊗g H →g G where\n  toFun := Prod.fst\n  map_rel' h := h.1\n\n\nThe second projection is a graph homomorphism G ⊗g H →g H./-- The second projection is a graph homomorphism G ⊗g H →g H. -/\ndef tensorProd.sndHom {V W : Type*} {G : SimpleGraph V} {H : SimpleGraph W} :\n    G ⊗g H →g H where\n  toFun := Prod.snd\n  map_rel' h := h.2\n\n\nPairing two homomorphisms f : K →g G and g : K →g H gives a homomorphism into\nthe tensor product. This is the universal property of the product./-- Pairing two homomorphisms f : K →g G and g : K →g H gives a homomorphism into\nthe tensor product. This is the universal property of the product. -/\ndef tensorProd.prod {V W X : Type*} {G : SimpleGraph V} {H : SimpleGraph W}\n    {K : SimpleGraph X} (f : K →g G) (g : K →g H) : K →g G ⊗g H where\n  toFun x := (f x, g x)\n  map_rel' h := ⟨f.map_adj h, g.map_adj h⟩\n\n\nComposing a colouring of G with the projection tensorProd.fstHom gives a\ncolouring of G ⊗g H, and similarly for H. So the chromatic number of G ⊗g H is\nat most the minimum of the chromatic numbers of G and H.\n\nexample {V W : Type*} {G : SimpleGraph V} {H : SimpleGraph W} {n : ℕ}\n    (h : G.Colorable n) : (G ⊗g H).Colorable n :=\n  Colorable.of_hom tensorProd.fstHom h\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Maps\u0009Tensor Products*","header":"5.7.1. Maps of Tensor Products","id":"/Maps/#section-maps-of-tensor-products"},"/Walks/#section-support-and-getvert":{"contents":"The support of a walk is the sequence of vertices it visits in order, as a\nList V.\n\n#source Walk.support\n\n\nA nil walk has length 0 and support [u]. So in particular every walk has at\nleast one vertex in its support. (Note that if walks were defined in terms of lists\nof vertices then we would have to decide what to do with the empty list.)\n\nexample : (Walk.nil : G.Walk u u).support = [u] := rfl\n\n\nThe support of a cons walk consists of the first vertex of the new edge followed by\nthe support of the rest of the walk. (Note the use of List notation here: a :: l\nis the list with head a and tail l).\n\nexample (h : G.Adj u v) (p : G.Walk v w) :\n    (Walk.cons h p).support = u :: p.support := Walk.support_cons h p\n\n\nA walk is determined by its support, so nothing is lost compared with the list-based\nmodel we considered at the start of the chapter.\n\n#check Walk.ext_support -- (h : p.support = q.support) : p = q\n\n\nThe start and end vertices of a walk belong to its support. Results involving the\nstart and end of a walk use \"start\" and \"end\" in their names, but Walk.start and\nWalk.end are not defined. (By design, if we have a walk p : G.Walk u v then u\nand v are already available as part of the type of p so we don't need special\nnames for them.)\n\n#check Walk.start_mem_support  -- (p : G.Walk u v) : u ∈ p.support\n#check Walk.end_mem_support    -- (p : G.Walk u v) : v ∈ p.support\n\n\nThe support of the walk pathNat.path i j from i to i + j in pathNat is\n[i, i + 1, ..., i + j], which can be expressed as List.range' i (j + 1).\n\n#check List.range'\n\n\n@[simp]\nlemma pathNat.support_path (i j : ℕ) :\n    (pathNat.path i j).support = List.range' i (j + 1) := by\n  induction j with\n  | zero => simp [pathNat.path]\n  | succ n ih => simp [pathNat.path, support_concat, ih, List.range'_1_concat]\n\n\nThe i-th vertex of a walk can be obtained with getVert.\n\n#source Walk.getVert\n\n\nIf p : G.Walk u v and i ≤ p.length, then p.getVert i is the i-th vertex in\nthe support of the walk.\n\nexample (p : G.Walk u v) : p.getVert 0 = u := p.getVert_zero\n\n\nexample (i : ℕ) : (Walk.nil : G.Walk u u).getVert i = u := Walk.getVert_nil u\n\n\nexample (h : G.Adj u v) (p : G.Walk v w) : (Walk.cons h p).getVert 1 = v := by\n  rw [getVert_cons_succ, getVert_zero]\n\n\nNotice that getVert i is well-defined for any i : ℕ, rather than requiring us to\ncheck that i ≤ p.length, it simply returns the end vertex if p.length ≤ i.\n\nexample (p : G.Walk v w) : p.getVert (p.length + 100) = w :=\n  p.getVert_of_length_le (by simp)\n\n\nIf i < p.length then p.getVert i and p.getVert (i + 1) are adjacent.\n\nexample {i} {p : G.Walk u v} (hi : i < p.length) :\n    G.Adj (p.getVert i) (p.getVert (i + 1)) := p.adj_getVert_succ hi\n\n\nWe have snd and penultimate as abbreviations for p.getVert 1 and\np.getVert (p.length - 1) respectively.\n\n#source Walk.snd         -- p.getVert 1\n#source Walk.penultimate -- p.getVert (p.length - 1)\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Walks\u0009Vertices and Edges of a Walk","header":"8.2.1. Support and getVert","id":"/Walks/#section-support-and-getvert"}});